    "raw": "# Model assignment for AC-gated WorkItems (orchestrator vs implementor)\n\nDecision framework for which model tier to put in each role of a todo/WorkItem\nworkflow, given that each WorkItem carries a clear, programmatically-checked AC\n(a unit/integration test).\n\n## Setup: the two roles have opposite trust profiles\n\nThe AC-as-test is a cheap oracle. It guards exactly one of the two roles.\n\n- **Implementor** (columns): takes one WorkItem, codes until its AC test is green.\n  **Guarded** by the oracle -- automatic, cheap. A weak model is tolerable because it\n  just iterates to green, and the full suite + lint catch collateral breakage. This is\n  the token-heavy role (iterate-to-green, verbose edit/test churn, isolated context), so\n  it is the **cost lever**. Trust requirement: low -- the test carries it.\n- **Orchestrator** (rows): decomposes the goal, **writes the ACs**, sequences, integrates.\n  **Unguarded** -- nothing automatically checks whether the decomposition is complete or\n  whether each AC captures the real requirement vs. a tautology. A wrong AC yields a green,\n  confident, wrong result: the oracle is poisoned at the source. This sets the **trust\n  floor**. Trust requirement: high.\n\nSo model quality buys the most on the orchestrator (unchecked judgment); model cheapness\nis safe on the implementor (backstopped, high volume). That inverts the common\n\"cheap orchestrator, expensive coder\" habit.\n\n## The 3x3 grid\n\nOrchestrator (rows) = trust floor. Implementor (columns) = cost lever. Sonnet is the\ntuning tier on both axes: a cheaper-than-Opus but still-sound AC author, and a\ngames-less / fewer-iterations step up from Haiku.\n\n```\n                    IMPL: OPUS            IMPL: SONNET          IMPL: HAIKU\n                +---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+\n ORCH: OPUS     | safe, wasteful      | HIGH-ASSURANCE ***  | LEAN VALUE ***      |\n (trust HIGH)   | WIN qual / LOSE $   | WIN / WIN           | WIN / WIN           |\n                | ~8-15x              | ~4-6x               | ~1.5-2.5x           |\n                +---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+\n ORCH: SONNET   | mismatched          | BALANCED default    | BUDGET default ***  |\n (trust MED-HI) | pay up on guarded   | WIN / WIN-ish       | WIN $ / OK trust    |\n                | ~7-13x              | ~3.5-5x             | ~1.3-1.8x           |\n                +---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+\n ORCH: HAIKU    | WORST (usual habit) | shaky floor         | trivial only        |\n (trust LOW)    | LOSE / LOSE         | good code / bad AC  | WIN $ / LOSE trust  |\n                | ~6-12x              | ~3-4x               | 1x                  |\n                +---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+\n   (cost bands are ballpark, baseline HH = 1x; implementor tier dominates $)\n```\n\n## Row breakdowns\n\n**ORCH: OPUS -- high trust floor, ACs sound + complete**\n- Impl Opus: no gaming, best on unchecked dims, fewest iterations -- but you pay top price\n  on the role the oracle already guards. Overkill.\n- Impl Sonnet: high-assurance pick. Strong ACs + an implementor that won't do something\n  dumb-but-green in the dimensions the AC can't test (perf, security, collateral breakage).\n  Best when AC coverage is imperfect.\n- Impl Haiku: max raw value. Cheapest heavy role under the strongest oracle. Needs the\n  guards (frozen AC, diff review, full suite) or Haiku games the test.\n\n**ORCH: SONNET -- medium-high floor, ACs mostly sound (may miss subtle/ambiguous edges)**\n- Impl Opus: mismatched -- spending up on the guarded role while the floor is only medium.\n- Impl Sonnet: balanced everyday default. No strong model anywhere, but Sonnet is capable\n  on both ends; fine for typical, moderately-clear work.\n- Impl Haiku: budget default. Best $/trust when requirements are clear enough that Sonnet\n  writes solid ACs. The value knee of the whole grid.\n\n**ORCH: HAIKU -- low trust floor, ACs often weak/wrong on anything non-trivial**\n- Impl Opus: worst -- great code faithfully implementing bad ACs -> green, confident, wrong,\n  and the priciest line. The common habit.\n- Impl Sonnet: good code on shaky ACs -- same silent-failure disease, cheaper.\n- Impl Haiku: cheapest; wrong ACs and gamed. Trivial/mechanical only.\n\n## Governing principle\n\n**Spend UP the rows, coast DOWN the columns.** Every dollar in the orchestrator row raises\nthe unguarded floor; the implementor column is backstopped, so you can descend it for cost.\nGood region = top rows. The bottom row is a defect factory no matter how much you spend\nacross it.\n\n## Picks\n\n- Clear, well-specified work -> **Sonnet orch / Haiku impl** (budget knee).\n- Subtle or ambiguous requirements (AC authorship is the real risk) -> **Opus orch**, and\n  choose the implementor by how much the ACs can't cover: **Haiku** if coverage is tight,\n  **Sonnet** if it isn't.\n- Reserve **Opus impl** for the rare case where AC-passing code still needs a strong hand\n  (concurrency, security, hairy refactors) -- and only ever paired with an Opus orchestrator.\n\n## Guards that make a cheap implementor safe\n\nThe whole scheme depends on the oracle not being gamed or bypassed:\n- **Freeze the AC test as read-only for the implementor.** The cheapest path to green is to\n  edit/skip/weaken the test. The orchestrator authors the AC into the WorkItem; the\n  implementor may not touch test files.\n- **Verify against the AC as authored** + full suite + lint, and **eyeball the diff** -- not\n  just the green check.\n- **Externalize plan state to todo.py** (on disk), not the orchestrator's context, so an\n  Opus orchestrator stays affordable (it queries state instead of carrying it)."
  },
